[interviewer · RECOMMENDATION · 12:52] That's your recommendation. A couple of pressure tests. Your whole pricing case rests on prices being flat while inputs inflated — what's the single biggest risk that undoes that logic?

[candidate · 14:22] The biggest risk is that the flat pricing was a deliberate choice, not an oversight — that Brew & Bean held prices because the market wouldn't bear more, and the 15% revenue growth is exactly what flat prices bought them. If that's true, raising 10% doesn't recover margin, it just trades volume for price and could leave absolute profit flat or worse.

And there's an arithmetic version of that risk. Si el volumen cae lo suficiente, I lose the fixed-cost absorption too — labor and rent at store level don't shrink with a 10% traffic drop, so labor could climb above 22% of revenue and eat the gain.

The thing that would settle it is understanding where the 15% growth came from — new stores versus same-store traffic. I asked early and we didn't get there. If it's mostly new units, same-store demand may be soft, and I'd be much more cautious on price.
